        <!-- 1. Problem & Outcomes -->
        <section id="problem">
          <h2>1. Problem &amp; Outcomes</h2>

          <h3>1.1 The Problem</h3>
          <p>Most interview processes silently leak:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Candidates no-show or drop after scheduling.</li>
            <li>Interviewers don’t submit scorecards on time.</li>
            <li>Hiring managers forget to deliver feedback.</li>
            <li>Candidates get inconsistent instructions across stages.</li>
            <li>Recruiters manually chase everyone, all the time.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>This creates longer time-to-fill, slower decisions, inconsistent experience, and constant operational drag.</p>

          <h3>1.2 The Outcomes We’re Aiming For</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Clean, predictable interview flows from screen → offer.</li>
            <li>Scorecards submitted within agreed-upon SLAs.</li>
            <li>Fewer no-shows and fewer “I never heard back” complaints.</li>
            <li>Standard, role-appropriate candidate communications.</li>
            <li>Recruiters focusing on real conversations, not inbox herding.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- 2. Scope & Design Principles -->
        <section id="scope">
          <h2>2. Scope &amp; Design Principles</h2>

          <h3>2.1 In-Scope</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Automation &amp; templates from recruiter screen through offer/pre-start.</li>
            <li>Scheduling workflows and reminders for candidates &amp; interviewers.</li>
            <li>Scorecard nudges, SLAs, and escalation rules.</li>
            <li>Status and delay communications (“we’re still here” messages).</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>2.2 Out-of-Scope (v1)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Top-funnel application review / screening logic (owned by Screening A[i]gent).</li>
            <li>Comp approvals, offer generation, and HRIS provisioning.</li>
            <li>Performance management or onboarding workflows.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>2.3 Design Principles</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Workflow-first, tool-second.</strong> Everything is designed around how recruiting really works, not just features available in the ATS.</li>
            <li><strong>Human-in-the-loop.</strong> Automation nudges and confirms; humans still make hiring decisions.</li>
            <li><strong>Configurable &amp; role-aware.</strong> Messaging and timing flex by role family and seniority.</li>
            <li><strong>Candidates never feel ghosted.</strong> Silence is considered a defect to design around.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>2.4 Global Rules</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>No automation that sends conflicting messages (e.g., double confirmations).</li>
            <li>Every automated message has a clear owner and a place to edit content.</li>
            <li>Templates support regional/legal nuance where required.</li>
            <li>All flows are logged somewhere visible to Recruiting Ops.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- 3. Roles & Responsibilities -->
        <section id="roles">
          <h2>3. Roles &amp; Responsibilities</h2>

          <h3>3.1 Recruiting Operations / TA Lead</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Owns the overall workflow design and SLAs.</li>
            <li>Configures automations inside the ATS and related tools.</li>
            <li>Monitors metrics and tunes workflows over time.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>3.2 Recruiters &amp; Coordinators</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Trigger the right workflows by moving candidates to the correct stages.</li>
            <li>Monitor exceptions (edge cases, reschedules, or escalations).</li>
            <li>Own candidate experience in 1:1 communication.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>3.3 Hiring Managers &amp; Interviewers</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Provide availability and commit to SLAs for scorecards.</li>
            <li>Respond to escalations when they’re overdue.</li>
            <li>Participate in continuous improvement conversations.</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="callout">
            <strong>Key idea:</strong> Workflow A[i]gent doesn’t replace humans; it makes it structurally easier
            for them to do the right thing on time.
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- 4. System Overview -->
        <section id="overview">
          <h2>4. System Overview</h2>

          <p>At a high level, Workflow A[i]gent delivers four major capability areas:</p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>Scheduling workflows</strong> – availability capture, reminders if candidates don’t respond, and alerts when threads stall.</li>
            <li><strong>Stage-based interview workflows</strong> – confirmations, reminders, and follow-ups for each interview stage.</li>
            <li><strong>SLAs &amp; escalations</strong> – scorecard due dates, automatic nudges, and escalation paths when people are late.</li>
            <li><strong>Status &amp; delay updates</strong> – structured check-ins when things are taking longer than usual.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>4.1 ATS Implementation (Conceptual)</h3>
          <p>
            The playbook assumes a modern ATS (e.g., Greenhouse) with the ability to:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Trigger emails or tasks based on stage change and event dates.</li>
            <li>Reference templates that can be configured per office / department.</li>
            <li>Run rules based on “time since last event” (e.g., 48 hours after interview).</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Where the ATS can’t do something directly (e.g., complex routing or cross-role logic), a lightweight
            automation layer (Zapier, Make, or internal tooling) can sit on top.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 5. Workflow Library -->
        <section id="library">
          <h2>5. Workflow Library</h2>

          <p>This library captures the core flows most teams need. Each can be adapted to your stages and naming.</p>

          <h3>5.1 Trigger Glossary</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>On stage change</strong> – Candidate moves into Screen / HM / Panel / Final / Offer.</li>
            <li><strong>On event booked</strong> – Interview scheduled on calendar.</li>
            <li><strong>On time since interview</strong> – X hours after scheduled interview end.</li>
            <li><strong>On inactivity</strong> – No update after Y days in stage.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>5.2 Scheduling &amp; Availability</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Availability capture email when candidate first reaches “Interview” stages.</li>
            <li>Reminder nudge if no response after 24–48 hours.</li>
            <li>Alert to recruiter when candidate has not booked after threshold time.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>5.3 Interview Stage Workflows</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>For each stage</strong> (Screen, HM, Panel, Final):</li>
            <li>Confirmation email with logistics + expectations.</li>
            <li>Reminder 24 hours before interview.</li>
            <li>Optional same-day reminder for virtual panels.</li>
            <li>Follow-up “thank you” with next-steps expectations.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>5.4 Scorecard &amp; SLA Automation</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Scorecard due within X hours after interview end.</li>
            <li>First nudge at X+24h if still incomplete.</li>
            <li>Escalation to hiring manager or recruiting lead at X+48h.</li>
            <li>Weekly digest of overdue scorecards for TA leadership.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>5.5 Status &amp; Delay Updates</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>“We’re still here” messages when assessments or panels take longer than usual.</li>
            <li>Templates for roles on hold, HM travel, or internal reprioritization.</li>
            <li>Optional branded “what’s happening behind the scenes” notes for senior candidates.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>5.6 Offer &amp; Pre-Start Workflows</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Offer scheduling &amp; confirmation email.</li>
            <li>“What to expect” between accept and start (IT, onboarding, paperwork).</li>
            <li>Check-ins at 1–2 touchpoints before day one.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- 6. Implementation -->
        <section id="implementation">
          <h2>6. Configuration &amp; Implementation Notes</h2>

          <p>Typical implementation steps:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Map current stages → Screen, HM/Tech, Panel, Final, Offer.</li>
            <li>Define SLAs for each stage (scorecards, response times, etc.).</li>
            <li>Create a template library (confirmation, reminder, follow-up, delay, regret).</li>
            <li>Wire triggers using ATS automation features:
              <ul>
                <li>On stage change</li>
                <li>On event booked</li>
                <li>On time since interview</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
            <li>Pilot on 1–2 roles (e.g., Sales + Eng) before expanding.</li>
            <li>Train recruiters, coordinators, and HMs on what’s automated vs. manual.</li>
            <li>Monitor metrics and tune: no-show rate, SLA adherence, average days per stage.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>6.1 Role-Based Customization</h3>
          <p>Most teams need at least light customization by role family:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Sales</strong> – heavier pre-interview prep, panel logistics, and expectation setting.</li>
            <li><strong>Engineering</strong> – linkage to technical assessments, repos, or coding exercises.</li>
            <li><strong>Exec / Leadership</strong> – tailored messages about stakeholders and decision timelines.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>6.2 ATS Configuration Example (Simplified)</h3>
          <p>
            In Greenhouse, for example, most of this playbook can be implemented with
            stage-change automations, email templates, and reminder rules layered on top of the existing pipeline.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 7. Metrics -->
        <section id="metrics">
          <h2>7. Metrics &amp; Continuous Improvement</h2>

          <p>Workflow A[i]gent isn’t “set and forget.” It’s a living system that improves as you learn.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>No-show rate by stage.</li>
            <li>Scorecard SLA adherence (% on time, by role / interviewer).</li>
            <li>Average days per stage (before and after automation).</li>
            <li>Candidate satisfaction / NPS, where measured.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            These metrics can be monitored by the Metrics A[i]gent and surfaced to TA leadership in regular reviews,
            closing the loop between design, automation, and outcomes.
          </p>
        </section>

